/* PsychLogic — Clinical Mock 10 (Neuropsychology · Neurodevelopment · Interventions). Question bank (verbatim from source doc). */
window.MOCK_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "question": "A therapy in which older adults use photos, music and objects to talk about their past experiences, often linked with Robert Butler’s idea of ‘life review’, is called:", "options": ["Reality orientation", "Reminiscence therapy", "Exposure therapy", "Aversion therapy"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Reminiscence therapy uses past memories to improve mood, well-being and communication in older people, including people with dementia. Butler (1963) described life review as a natural process in old age.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Reality orientation focuses on the present (date, place).</li><li>Exposure therapy treats fear by facing the feared stimulus.</li><li>Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour with something unpleasant.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Erikson’s stage of integrity versus despair is linked with life review.</li><li>Butler also coined the term ‘ageism’ (1969).</li><li>Long-term memories are often better preserved than recent ones in dementia.</li><li>A patient with paralysis of his left arm and leg insists that nothing is wrong with him. Unawareness or denial of one’s own deficit is called:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 2, "question": "A patient with paralysis of his left arm and leg insists that nothing is wrong with him. Unawareness or denial of one’s own deficit is called:", "options": ["Anhedonia", "Alexithymia", "Anosognosia", "Agnosia"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Anosognosia (Babinski, 1914) is the lack of awareness of one’s own illness or deficit. It is common after right parietal damage, especially for left-sided paralysis.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Agnosia is failure to recognise objects despite intact senses.</li><li>Alexithymia is difficulty identifying and describing one’s own feelings.</li><li>Anhedonia is loss of pleasure.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Anosognosia also occurs in Anton’s syndrome (denial of blindness).</li><li>It is a neurological lack of insight, not psychological denial.</li><li>It makes rehabilitation harder.</li><li>Ivar Lovaas’s 1987 study, which reported large gains in young autistic children given about 40 hours a week of one-to-one therapy, used:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GMCH 2023. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 3, "question": "Ivar Lovaas’s 1987 study, which reported large gains in young autistic children given about 40 hours a week of one-to-one therapy, used:", "options": ["Psychoanalytic play therapy", "Sensory integration therapy", "Facilitated communication", "Early intensive behavioural intervention based on applied behaviour analysis"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> O. Ivar Lovaas (UCLA) used intensive applied behaviour analysis (ABA), mainly discrete trial training, from a young age. He reported that about 47% of the intensive group reached normal intellectual and educational functioning.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Psychoanalytic play therapy has little evidence in autism.</li><li>Sensory integration therapy was developed by Jean Ayres.</li><li>Facilitated communication has been discredited.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ABA applies operant principles: reinforcement, prompting, shaping, chaining.</li><li>Later naturalistic approaches include Pivotal Response Treatment and the Early Start Denver Model.</li><li>The original study did not use random assignment.</li><li>Which statement about language lateralisation in left-handed people is correct?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 4, "question": "Which statement about language lateralisation in left-handed people is correct?", "options": ["Most left-handers still have language mainly in the left hemisphere", "Handedness completely decides which hemisphere controls language", "Almost all left-handers have language in the right hemisphere", "Left-handers always have language in both hemispheres equally"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Wada-test studies (Rasmussen and Milner, 1977) found that about 70% of left-handers have left-hemisphere language, about 15% right and about 15% bilateral. In right-handers, about 95–96% are left-dominant.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Only a minority of left-handers are right-dominant.</li><li>Bilateral language occurs in only some left-handers.</li><li>Handedness and language dominance are linked, but not completely.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RML 2024 asked whether the left hemisphere is called the ‘major’ or ‘minor’ hemisphere.</li><li>The right hemisphere is stronger for spatial skills, faces and prosody.</li><li>JANAS: ‘Left hemisphere is to right hemisphere as language functioning is to spatial…’</li><li>The most common preventable (non-genetic) cause of intellectual disability is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 5, "question": "The most common preventable (non-genetic) cause of intellectual disability is:", "options": ["Rett syndrome", "Fragile X syndrome", "Cerebral palsy", "Fetal alcohol syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Fetal alcohol syndrome results from alcohol use during pregnancy. It causes small head size, a smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, growth problems and intellectual and behavioural difficulties — and it is fully preventable.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Fragile X is genetic, so it is not preventable this way.</li><li>Cerebral palsy is a motor disorder; many people with it have normal intelligence.</li><li>Rett syndrome is genetic.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The wider condition is called fetal alcohol spectrum disorder (FASD).</li><li>No safe amount of alcohol in pregnancy is known.</li><li>Other preventable causes include iodine deficiency and birth asphyxia.</li><li>Before epilepsy surgery, a neurosurgeon wants to find out which hemisphere controls a patient’s language. The classic test, which briefly puts one hemisphere ‘to sleep’ with a drug, is the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RML 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 6, "question": "Before epilepsy surgery, a neurosurgeon wants to find out which hemisphere controls a patient’s language. The classic test, which briefly puts one hemisphere ‘to sleep’ with a drug, is the:", "options": ["Wada test", "Dichotic listening test", "Halstead Category Test", "Bender Gestalt test"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In the Wada test (Juhn Wada, 1949), sodium amobarbital is injected into one internal carotid artery, briefly silencing that hemisphere. If the patient cannot speak, that side is dominant for language.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Dichotic listening gives a non-invasive estimate of language dominance but is not a drug test.</li><li>The Category Test measures abstract reasoning.</li><li>The Bender test measures visuomotor ability.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Functional MRI is now often used instead of the Wada test.</li><li>The test also checks memory support on each side before temporal lobe surgery.</li><li>About 95% of right-handers have left-hemisphere language.</li><li>All of the following are listed under ‘restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour’ for autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 7, "question": "All of the following are listed under ‘restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour’ for autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Highly restricted, fixated interests", "Insistence on sameness and rigid routines", "Deficits in non-verbal communication such as eye contact", "Unusual reaction to sensory input"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Deficits in non-verbal communication belong to the social-communication domain (Criterion A), not to the RRB domain (Criterion B).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Insistence on sameness is an RRB.</li><li>Fixated interests are an RRB.</li><li>Sensory hyper- or hypo-reactivity was added to the RRBs in DSM-5.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-IV had three domains; DSM-5 merged social and communication into one.</li><li>RINPAS 2024 asked which behaviours are NOT included in DSM-5 ASD.</li><li>Lining up toys and echolalia are examples of RRBs.</li><li>Mrs. Bose, 72, has memory and attention problems that vary sharply from day to day. She sees detailed people and animals that are not there, and she has mild stiffness and slowness. She had a severe reaction to an antipsychotic. The most likely diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 8, "question": "Mrs. Bose, 72, has memory and attention problems that vary sharply from day to day. She sees detailed people and animals that are not there, and she has mild stiffness and slowness. She had a severe reaction to an antipsychotic. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Frontotemporal dementia", "Dementia with Lewy bodies", "Delirium due to infection", "Alzheimer’s disease"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The core features of dementia with Lewy bodies are fluctuating cognition, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, parkinsonism and REM sleep behaviour disorder. Severe sensitivity to antipsychotics is a key supportive feature.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Alzheimer’s disease rarely shows early hallucinations and parkinsonism.</li><li>FTD starts with behaviour or language change.</li><li>Delirium develops over hours to days, not as a long-standing dementia.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Lewy bodies contain alpha-synuclein.</li><li>Parkinson’s disease dementia is diagnosed if dementia comes more than a year after the motor symptoms.</li><li>Cholinesterase inhibitors often help.</li><li>Uta Frith’s idea that people with autism tend to process details rather than the overall meaning or ‘big picture’ is called:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2025. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 9, "question": "Uta Frith’s idea that people with autism tend to process details rather than the overall meaning or ‘big picture’ is called:", "options": ["Weak central coherence", "Executive dysfunction", "Extreme male brain", "Mindblindness"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Weak central coherence (Frith, 1989) explains why autistic people may excel at spotting details (e.g. embedded figures) but miss the overall context.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Executive dysfunction theory explains rigidity and poor planning.</li><li>Mindblindness refers to a theory-of-mind deficit.</li><li>The extreme male brain theory is Baron-Cohen’s.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Autistic people often do well on the Embedded Figures and Block Design tests.</li><li>Later work speaks of a detail-focused ‘cognitive style’ rather than a deficit.</li><li>These three cognitive theories are often compared in exams.</li><li>The Portage Guide to Early Education, widely used in India for home-based early intervention for children with developmental delay, mainly works by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 10, "question": "The Portage Guide to Early Education, widely used in India for home-based early intervention for children with developmental delay, mainly works by:", "options": ["Admitting the child to a residential school", "Training parents to teach their child skills at home using a checklist of developmental goals", "Providing medicines to speed up brain development", "Using psychoanalytic play interpretation"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Portage model (from Portage, Wisconsin, around 1969–72) uses a developmental checklist across infant stimulation, socialisation, language, self-help, cognition and motor areas. Home visitors train parents to teach the next skills through small, reinforced steps.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>There is no medicine for this.</li><li>It is home-based, not residential.</li><li>It uses behavioural, not psychoanalytic, methods.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Early intervention is most effective in the first years of life.</li><li>Indian versions have been widely used by NIMH (Secunderabad) and NGOs.</li><li>DST and VSMS help track progress.</li><li>A 2-year-old girl developed normally at first, but then lost her purposeful hand use and began constant hand-wringing. Her head growth slowed and she lost her few words. A mutation in which gene is most likely?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RML 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 11, "question": "A 2-year-old girl developed normally at first, but then lost her purposeful hand use and began constant hand-wringing. Her head growth slowed and she lost her few words. A mutation in which gene is most likely?", "options": ["HTT", "FMR1", "MECP2", "APP"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> This is Rett syndrome: almost always in girls, normal early development, then loss of hand skills with stereotyped hand-wringing, slowing of head growth, loss of speech and gait problems. It is caused by mutations in the X-linked MECP2 gene.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>FMR1 causes fragile X syndrome.</li><li>APP is linked to Alzheimer’s disease.</li><li>HTT is the Huntington’s disease gene.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Andreas Rett described it in 1966.</li><li>DSM-IV listed Rett’s disorder as a pervasive developmental disorder; DSM-5 removed it.</li><li>RINPAS 2024 listed Rett’s with Asperger’s and childhood disintegrative disorder.</li><li>In constraint-induced movement therapy for stroke patients, the healthy arm is restrained so that the patient must use the weak arm. This is mainly designed to overcome:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RINPAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 12, "question": "In constraint-induced movement therapy for stroke patients, the healthy arm is restrained so that the patient must use the weak arm. This is mainly designed to overcome:", "options": ["Conversion paralysis", "Muscle wasting from lack of food", "Hemispatial neglect of the right side", "Learned non-use of the affected limb"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Edward Taub found that after a stroke, patients learn to rely on the good arm, so the weak arm is used even less (‘learned non-use’). Forcing intensive use of the weak arm reverses this and promotes neuroplasticity.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Conversion paralysis is a functional neurological symptom, not the target.</li><li>Neglect is a different problem and is usually of the left side.</li><li>Muscle wasting from poor food intake is not the target.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Taub’s work grew out of deafferentation studies in monkeys.</li><li>Brain imaging shows cortical reorganisation after CIMT.</li><li>Mirror therapy is another stroke rehabilitation method.</li><li>When asked to touch his nose and then the doctor’s finger, a patient overshoots the target and his hand shakes more as it gets closer. His gait is wide-based and unsteady. The damage is most likely in the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RML 2024, GMCH 2023, JEMAS 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 13, "question": "When asked to touch his nose and then the doctor’s finger, a patient overshoots the target and his hand shakes more as it gets closer. His gait is wide-based and unsteady. The damage is most likely in the:", "options": ["Thalamus", "Primary motor cortex", "Basal ganglia", "Cerebellum"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Overshooting (dysmetria), tremor that increases near the target (intention tremor) and unsteady, wide-based gait (ataxia) are classic signs of cerebellar damage.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Basal ganglia damage gives resting tremor, rigidity or involuntary movements.</li><li>Motor cortex damage gives weakness of the opposite side.</li><li>Thalamic damage mainly affects sensation.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Chronic alcohol use often damages the cerebellar vermis, causing gait ataxia.</li><li>Other cerebellar signs: slurred (scanning) speech, nystagmus.</li><li>The cerebellum also supports procedural learning and timing.</li><li>Attention Process Training, a structured programme to retrain sustained, selective, alternating and divided attention after brain injury, was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, RINPAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 14, "question": "Attention Process Training, a structured programme to retrain sustained, selective, alternating and divided attention after brain injury, was developed by:", "options": ["McKay Sohlberg and Catherine Mateer", "Edward Taub", "Charles Golden", "Barbara Wilson and Alan Baddeley"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Sohlberg and Mateer (1987) developed Attention Process Training (APT), based on their clinical model of attention with focused, sustained, selective, alternating and divided levels.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Taub developed constraint-induced movement therapy.</li><li>Wilson and Baddeley are linked with errorless learning.</li><li>Golden developed the Luria–Nebraska battery.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>APT is a restorative approach.</li><li>Goal Management Training (Robertson) targets executive dysfunction.</li><li>Computer-based cognitive training is a modern extension.</li><li>Mr. Kapoor can still write a letter after his stroke, but a few minutes later he cannot read what he has written. His speech is normal. This condition is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GMCH 2023, JEMAS 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 15, "question": "Mr. Kapoor can still write a letter after his stroke, but a few minutes later he cannot read what he has written. His speech is normal. This condition is:", "options": ["Gerstmann syndrome", "Alexia without agraphia (pure alexia)", "Wernicke’s aphasia", "Alexia with agraphia"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Pure alexia (Dejerine, 1892) usually follows damage to the left occipital lobe and the splenium of the corpus callosum. Visual words cannot reach the left-hemisphere language areas, but writing, which does not need vision of the words, is spared.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Alexia with agraphia follows angular gyrus damage — both reading and writing are lost.</li><li>Wernicke’s aphasia affects comprehension of speech too.</li><li>Gerstmann syndrome includes agraphia, so writing would be impaired.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Pure alexia is a classic ‘disconnection syndrome’ (Geschwind, 1965).</li><li>Patients often read letter by letter.</li><li>Acquired alexia differs from developmental dyslexia.</li><li>Match the term (List I) with the main area of difficulty (List II):</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GBU 2025, JEMAS 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 16, "question": "Match the term (List I) with the main area of difficulty (List II): Codes:<div class=\"mt-wrap\"><table class=\"match-table\"><thead><tr><th>List I</th><th>List II</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Dyslexia</td><td>a. Motor planning and coordination</td></tr><tr><td>2. Dysgraphia</td><td>b. Mathematics and number sense</td></tr><tr><td>3. Dyscalculia</td><td>c. Written expression and handwriting</td></tr><tr><td>4. Dyspraxia</td><td>d. Reading and word decoding</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "options": ["1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a", "1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-b", "1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a", "1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Dyslexia – reading; dysgraphia – writing; dyscalculia – mathematics; dyspraxia – motor planning and coordination (developmental coordination disorder in DSM-5).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The other codes swap reading with writing, or mathematics with motor coordination.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 specifiers: impairment in reading, in written expression, in mathematics.</li><li>GBU 2025 asked which is NOT a learning difficulty (ADHD).</li><li>Dyspraxia is not a learning disorder in DSM-5; it is a motor disorder.</li><li>Rekha speaks fluently and understands speech well, but when asked to repeat ‘The boy is going to school’, she makes many errors. Her aphasia is most likely:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GBU 2025, JEMAS 2022, M.Phil Entrance 2021. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 17, "question": "Rekha speaks fluently and understands speech well, but when asked to repeat ‘The boy is going to school’, she makes many errors. Her aphasia is most likely:", "options": ["Transcortical sensory aphasia", "Broca’s aphasia", "Conduction aphasia", "Anomic aphasia"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Conduction aphasia is marked by fluent speech, good comprehension but strikingly poor repetition. It is classically linked to damage to the arcuate fasciculus (or supramarginal gyrus), which links Wernicke’s and Broca’s areas.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Transcortical aphasias keep repetition intact.</li><li>Broca’s aphasia is non-fluent.</li><li>Anomic aphasia mainly causes word-finding problems; repetition is fine.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Repetition is the key bedside test: it is poor in Broca’s, Wernicke’s, conduction and global aphasia.</li><li>Repetition is preserved in transcortical and anomic aphasias.</li><li>Wernicke predicted conduction aphasia from his model (1874).</li><li>In a classroom token economy, Ramesh loses two tokens each time he hits a classmate. This procedure is called:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2025. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 18, "question": "In a classroom token economy, Ramesh loses two tokens each time he hits a classmate. This procedure is called:", "options": ["Response cost (a form of negative punishment)", "Extinction", "Positive punishment", "Negative reinforcement"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Response cost removes a reinforcer the person already has (tokens, points) after a problem behaviour. Taking something pleasant away to reduce a behaviour is negative punishment.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Positive punishment adds something unpleasant.</li><li>Negative reinforcement increases a behaviour by removing something unpleasant.</li><li>Extinction stops the reinforcer that maintains the behaviour.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Time-out is another form of negative punishment.</li><li>Response cost works well with token economies in ADHD.</li><li>Reinforcing good behaviour should outweigh penalties.</li><li>In retrograde amnesia after a head injury, memories from the distant past are usually better preserved than recent ones. This is known as:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 19, "question": "In retrograde amnesia after a head injury, memories from the distant past are usually better preserved than recent ones. This is known as:", "options": ["The serial position effect", "Ribot’s law", "The Zeigarnik effect", "Jost’s law"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Théodule Ribot (1882) noted that recent memories are lost first and older, well-consolidated memories survive best — a ‘temporal gradient’ of retrograde amnesia.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Jost’s law concerns how older and newer memories of equal strength decay.</li><li>The Zeigarnik effect is better recall of unfinished tasks.</li><li>The serial position effect is better recall of items at the start and end of a list.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The temporal gradient supports the idea of memory consolidation.</li><li>Retrograde = loss of memories before the injury; anterograde = inability to form new ones after it.</li><li>Post-traumatic amnesia duration helps grade head-injury severity.</li><li>In a case of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder, once the diagnosis is made, the following is true regarding management:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RML 2024, JEMAS 2024, JEMAS 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 20, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · GMCH 2023</span>In a case of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder, once the diagnosis is made, the following is true regarding management:", "options": ["Prolonged psychotherapy", "Cognitive processing therapy is useful", "Clonazepam is very useful", "Thought stopping is useful"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Trauma-focused psychological therapies — cognitive processing therapy (CPT), prolonged exposure, trauma-focused CBT and EMDR — are first-line treatments for PTSD. CPT helps the person challenge ‘stuck points’ such as self-blame.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Long, open-ended therapy is not the recommended first step; trauma-focused therapies are time-limited (about 12 sessions for CPT).</li><li>Benzodiazepines such as clonazepam are not recommended for PTSD and may hinder recovery.</li><li>Thought stopping is not an evidence-based PTSD treatment and can increase intrusions.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>CPT was developed by Patricia Resick.</li><li>Prolonged exposure was developed by Edna Foa.</li><li>SSRIs (sertraline, paroxetine) are the first-line medicines.</li><li>In a test, a child sees Sally put a marble in her basket and leave. Anne moves it to a box. Asked where Sally will look for the marble, 6-year-old Arjun, who has autism, points to the box. This suggests a difficulty with:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This is an actual previous-year question (GMCH 2023). Examiners do repeat questions, sometimes word for word, and this area also appeared in IHBAS 2025, IHBAS 2024, RML 2022. Make sure you can answer it instantly — and keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 21, "question": "In a test, a child sees Sally put a marble in her basket and leave. Anne moves it to a box. Asked where Sally will look for the marble, 6-year-old Arjun, who has autism, points to the box. This suggests a difficulty with:", "options": ["Object permanence", "Weak central coherence", "Conservation", "Theory of mind"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Sally–Anne false-belief test (Baron-Cohen, Leslie and Frith, 1985) checks whether a child understands that others can hold beliefs different from reality. Pointing to the box shows a failure to represent Sally’s false belief — a theory-of-mind deficit (‘mindblindness’).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Object permanence (Piaget) is knowing objects exist when hidden, gained in infancy.</li><li>Conservation is understanding that quantity stays the same when shape changes.</li><li>Weak central coherence is about detail-focused processing, not others’ beliefs.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Most typical children pass false-belief tests by about age 4.</li><li>Premack and Woodruff (1978) coined ‘theory of mind’.</li><li>Baron-Cohen also proposed the empathising–systemising theory.</li><li>Sensory integration therapy, often used with children with autism and developmental delays, was developed by the occupational therapist:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IMHANS 2025, GBU 2025, IHBAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 22, "question": "Sensory integration therapy, often used with children with autism and developmental delays, was developed by the occupational therapist:", "options": ["Maria Montessori", "Temple Grandin", "A. Jean Ayres", "Arnold Gesell"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A. Jean Ayres developed sensory integration theory and therapy in the 1960s–70s. It uses play activities (swings, textures, deep pressure) to help the brain organise sensory input.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Montessori developed an educational method using sensory materials.</li><li>Temple Grandin is an autistic scientist known for her ‘squeeze machine’.</li><li>Gesell developed developmental schedules.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Evidence for sensory integration therapy is mixed.</li><li>DSM-5 added sensory hyper- and hypo-reactivity to the autism criteria.</li><li>Occupational therapists also treat developmental coordination disorder.</li><li>The term ‘autism’ was first used in psychiatry (around 1911) to describe a symptom of schizophrenia by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 23, "question": "The term ‘autism’ was first used in psychiatry (around 1911) to describe a symptom of schizophrenia by:", "options": ["Eugen Bleuler", "Emil Kraepelin", "Leo Kanner", "Hans Asperger"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Eugen Bleuler used ‘autism’ for the schizophrenic withdrawal into one’s inner world — one of his ‘four A’s’. Leo Kanner (1943) later borrowed the word for ‘early infantile autism’, and Hans Asperger (1944) for ‘autistic psychopathy’.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Kanner first described infantile autism as a childhood condition (1943).</li><li>Asperger described milder cases in 1944.</li><li>Kraepelin described ‘dementia praecox’.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IHBAS 2025 asked who first described infantile autism (Kanner).</li><li>Bleuler’s four A’s: associations, affect, ambivalence, autism.</li><li>Kanner’s paper: ‘Autistic Disturbances of Affective Contact’.</li><li>Arrange the steps of Meichenbaum and Goodman’s self-instructional training for impulsive children in the correct order:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2025, RML 2024, RINPAS 2019. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 24, "question": "Arrange the steps of Meichenbaum and Goodman’s self-instructional training for impulsive children in the correct order: I. Covert self-instruction II. Cognitive modelling III. Overt self-guidance IV. Overt external guidance", "options": ["IV, II, III, I", "II, III, IV, I", "II, IV, I, III", "II, IV, III, I"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Self-instructional training (1971): (1) the adult models the task while talking aloud (cognitive modelling); (2) the child does it while the adult instructs (overt external guidance); (3) the child does it while instructing himself aloud (overt self-guidance); (4) the child whispers (faded); (5) the child guides himself silently (covert self-instruction).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The steps move from the adult’s speech to the child’s own silent inner speech.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The method draws on Vygotsky and Luria’s ideas about inner speech.</li><li>It is used for ADHD and impulsive behaviour.</li><li>Meichenbaum also developed stress inoculation training.</li><li>Twin studies estimate the heritability of ADHD at about:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 25, "question": "Twin studies estimate the heritability of ADHD at about:", "options": ["70–80%", "30–40%", "10–20%", "Close to 100%"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Twin and adoption studies (e.g. Faraone and colleagues) put ADHD heritability at about 74%, making it one of the most heritable psychiatric disorders. Environment still matters (e.g. prematurity, prenatal smoking).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>10–20% is far too low.</li><li>30–40% is too low.</li><li>No psychiatric disorder is fully (100%) genetic.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Heritability describes a population, not one person.</li><li>Many genes of small effect are involved.</li><li>Dopamine transporter (DAT1) and D4 receptor (DRD4) genes are commonly studied.</li><li>A functional behavioural assessment of a child’s self-injury looks for the function that keeps the behaviour going. All of the following are commonly identified functions, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 26, "question": "A functional behavioural assessment of a child’s self-injury looks for the function that keeps the behaviour going. All of the following are commonly identified functions, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Escape from demands or tasks", "Gaining attention from others", "Expression of an unconscious symbolic conflict", "Automatic (sensory) stimulation"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Functional behavioural assessment uses Antecedent–Behaviour–Consequence (ABC) records. The four common functions are attention, escape/avoidance, access to tangible items, and automatic (sensory) reinforcement. Unconscious symbolism is a psychodynamic idea, not a behavioural function.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Attention is a very common function.</li><li>Escape from demands is a very common function.</li><li>Automatic reinforcement is when the behaviour itself produces the reward (e.g. sensory input).</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The fourth common function is access to tangible items (toys, food).</li><li>Functional analysis (Iwata, 1982) tests functions experimentally.</li><li>Functional communication training replaces problem behaviour with communication that serves the same function.</li><li>Which of the following best describes a common characteristic of dyslexia?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 27, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · GBU 2025</span>Which of the following best describes a common characteristic of dyslexia?", "options": ["Difficulty understanding spoken language.", "Difficulty with reading and decoding words.", "Difficulty with motor coordination.", "Difficulty concentrating during lectures."], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Dyslexia is a specific learning disorder in reading: inaccurate or slow word reading and poor decoding (linking letters to sounds), despite adequate intelligence and teaching. A core problem is phonological processing.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Problems understanding speech suggest a language disorder.</li><li>Poor motor coordination suggests developmental coordination disorder.</li><li>Poor concentration suggests ADHD, though it can co-occur.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5: specific learning disorder ‘with impairment in reading’; ‘dyslexia’ is an accepted alternative term.</li><li>GBU 2025 also asked which is NOT a learning difficulty (ADHD).</li><li>Reading problems are commonly linked with left temporo-parietal areas.</li><li>‘Subcortical’ dementias such as those of Huntington’s and Parkinson’s disease typically show all of the following, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This is an actual previous-year question (GBU 2025). Examiners do repeat questions, sometimes word for word, and this area also appeared in M.Phil Entrance 2021, JEPAS 2020, RINPAS 2019. Make sure you can answer it instantly — and keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 28, "question": "‘Subcortical’ dementias such as those of Huntington’s and Parkinson’s disease typically show all of the following, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Apathy and low motivation", "Slowed thinking (bradyphrenia)", "Memory retrieval problems that improve with cues", "Early aphasia, apraxia and agnosia"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Subcortical dementia (Albert, 1974) shows slowed thinking, apathy, depression and retrieval-type memory problems helped by cues. The ‘cortical’ signs — aphasia, apraxia and agnosia — are typical of Alzheimer’s disease instead.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Bradyphrenia is a hallmark of subcortical dementia.</li><li>Apathy is very common in subcortical dementia.</li><li>Retrieval problems that improve with cues are typical of subcortical dementia.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Alzheimer’s disease shows storage (encoding) failure — cues do not help much.</li><li>Sundowning (worse confusion in the evening) is common in dementia; JEMAS 2024 asked about it.</li><li>HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder is also subcortical.</li><li>In DSM-5, specific learning disorder requires that the learning difficulties have persisted for at least how long, despite targeted help?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 29, "question": "In DSM-5, specific learning disorder requires that the learning difficulties have persisted for at least how long, despite targeted help?", "options": ["6 months", "3 months", "1 year", "2 years"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 requires at least 6 months of difficulty despite interventions targeting those problems. This reflects a ‘response to intervention’ approach.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>3 months is too short.</li><li>1 year is not the DSM-5 rule.</li><li>2 years is not the DSM-5 rule.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 combined reading, writing and mathematics into one diagnosis with specifiers.</li><li>An IQ–achievement discrepancy is no longer required.</li><li>Difficulties must not be better explained by ID, sensory problems or poor teaching.</li><li>The Luria–Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery, which standardised Luria’s methods into a scored test, was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, IHBAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 30, "question": "The Luria–Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery, which standardised Luria’s methods into a scored test, was developed by:", "options": ["Arthur Benton", "Charles Golden and colleagues", "Muriel Lezak", "Ralph Reitan and Ward Halstead"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Charles J. Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch (around 1978–1980) turned Luria’s qualitative bedside methods into a standardised battery with clinical scales such as motor, rhythm, tactile, visual, speech, writing, reading, arithmetic, memory and intellectual processes.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Halstead and Reitan developed the Halstead–Reitan Battery.</li><li>Benton developed tests such as the BVRT and Line Orientation.</li><li>Lezak wrote the standard textbook ‘Neuropsychological Assessment’.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Form I has 11 clinical scales; Form II adds an intermediate memory scale.</li><li>An old entrance paper asked how many clinical scales the Luria battery has.</li><li>The Halstead–Reitan Battery gives the Halstead Impairment Index.</li><li>All of the following are classified as neurodevelopmental disorders in DSM-5, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in M.Phil Entrance 2021. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 31, "question": "All of the following are classified as neurodevelopmental disorders in DSM-5, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Tourette’s disorder", "Specific learning disorder", "Developmental coordination disorder", "Selective mutism"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Selective mutism — consistently not speaking in certain social situations despite speaking elsewhere — is classified under anxiety disorders in DSM-5.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Specific learning disorder is neurodevelopmental.</li><li>Tic disorders, including Tourette’s, are neurodevelopmental motor disorders.</li><li>DCD is a neurodevelopmental motor disorder.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Separation anxiety disorder also moved to the anxiety chapter in DSM-5.</li><li>Neurodevelopmental groups: ID, communication, ASD, ADHD, SLD, motor disorders.</li><li>GBU 2025 asked which is NOT typically a learning difficulty.</li><li>A 65-year-old man has a slow, shuffling gait, a tremor of his hand at rest and stiffness of his limbs. These symptoms are mainly due to degeneration of dopamine neurons in the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 32, "question": "A 65-year-old man has a slow, shuffling gait, a tremor of his hand at rest and stiffness of his limbs. These symptoms are mainly due to degeneration of dopamine neurons in the:", "options": ["Locus coeruleus", "Hippocampus", "Substantia nigra", "Nucleus basalis of Meynert"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Parkinson’s disease results from loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, which project to the striatum. The cardinal signs are bradykinesia, resting tremor, rigidity and postural instability.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The hippocampus is involved in memory, not movement.</li><li>The locus coeruleus is the main noradrenaline nucleus.</li><li>The nucleus basalis of Meynert (acetylcholine) is affected in Alzheimer’s disease.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>JEPAS 2020 asked which disorder is linked with acetylcholine deficiency (Alzheimer’s).</li><li>Huntington’s disease affects the caudate nucleus.</li><li>L-dopa is the main drug treatment for Parkinson’s disease.</li><li>Cognitive remediation therapy in schizophrenia mainly aims to improve:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as RML 2024, JEMAS 2024, GMCH 2023 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 33, "question": "Cognitive remediation therapy in schizophrenia mainly aims to improve:", "options": ["Family expressed emotion", "Positive symptoms by removing delusions directly", "Medication adherence through injections", "Neurocognitive skills such as attention, memory and executive functions"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Cognitive remediation uses repeated, structured exercises (often computer-based) to improve attention, memory, processing speed and executive function, which predict real-world functioning in schizophrenia.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Delusions are targeted by CBT for psychosis, not mainly by remediation.</li><li>Expressed emotion is targeted by family interventions.</li><li>Long-acting injections are a medical approach to adherence.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Til Wykes is a key researcher in cognitive remediation.</li><li>It works best when combined with vocational rehabilitation.</li><li>Cognitive deficits are core features of schizophrenia.</li><li>A: Hemispatial neglect is more common and more severe after right-hemisphere damage than after left-hemisphere damage.  R: The right hemisphere attends to both sides of space, while the left hemisphere attends mainly to the right side.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IMHANS 2021. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 34, "question": "<p class=\"stem-lead\">Read the Assertion (A) and Reason (R) and choose the correct option:</p><div class=\"ar-block\"><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Assertion (A)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Hemispatial neglect is more common and more severe after right-hemisphere damage than after left-hemisphere damage.</span></div><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Reason (R)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">The right hemisphere attends to both sides of space, while the left hemisphere attends mainly to the right side.</span></div></div>", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Both are true, and R explains A. If the left hemisphere is damaged, the right hemisphere still covers both sides, so neglect is mild. If the right hemisphere is damaged, the left hemisphere covers only the right side, so the left side is ignored.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>B is wrong because R does explain A.</li><li>C and D are wrong because both statements are true.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>This is Heilman’s ‘hemispheric asymmetry’ account of attention.</li><li>Neglect is tested with line bisection, letter cancellation and clock drawing.</li><li>Extinction: a stimulus on the bad side is missed only when both sides are touched together.</li><li>For insomnia, Mr. Ali is told to use his bed only for sleep, to get up if he cannot sleep within about 20 minutes, and to wake at the same time every day. This is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 35, "question": "For insomnia, Mr. Ali is told to use his bed only for sleep, to get up if he cannot sleep within about 20 minutes, and to wake at the same time every day. This is:", "options": ["Sleep hygiene education only", "Progressive muscle relaxation", "Stimulus control therapy", "Paradoxical intention"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Richard Bootzin’s stimulus control therapy (1972) strengthens the bed as a cue for sleep and weakens its link with wakefulness and worry.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Sleep hygiene covers general habits (caffeine, exercise), not these specific rules.</li><li>Paradoxical intention asks the person to try to stay awake.</li><li>Progressive muscle relaxation (Jacobson) reduces bodily tension.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>CBT for insomnia (CBT-I) combines stimulus control, sleep restriction and cognitive work.</li><li>Sleep restriction limits time in bed to actual sleep time.</li><li>CBT-I is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia.</li><li>To diagnose autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5, how many of the four restricted, repetitive behaviour (RRB) symptoms must be present?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 36, "question": "To diagnose autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5, how many of the four restricted, repetitive behaviour (RRB) symptoms must be present?", "options": ["At least three", "At least two", "At least one", "All four"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 needs all three social-communication deficits (social-emotional reciprocity, non-verbal communication, relationships) and at least two of the four RRB symptoms.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>One RRB symptom is not enough.</li><li>Three are not required.</li><li>All four are not required.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The four RRBs: stereotyped movements/speech, insistence on sameness, fixated interests, sensory hyper- or hypo-reactivity.</li><li>Symptoms must be present in early development.</li><li>Severity is rated separately for social communication and RRBs.</li><li>Prosopagnosia results in an inability to:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 37, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IHBAS 2024</span>Prosopagnosia results in an inability to:", "options": ["Recognize faces", "Distinguish tastes", "Express feelings through facial expressions", "Recognize objects"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Prosopagnosia (‘face blindness’) is a visual agnosia for faces. The person sees the face clearly but cannot tell whose face it is, and often recognises people by voice, gait or clothes instead.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Loss of taste discrimination is not an agnosia of vision.</li><li>Difficulty expressing feelings on the face is a motor/emotional problem, not agnosia.</li><li>Inability to recognise objects is visual object agnosia, a broader problem.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>It is linked to damage of the fusiform gyrus (‘fusiform face area’), usually bilateral or right-sided.</li><li>Agnosia = failure to recognise despite intact senses.</li><li>Capgras delusion is the opposite pattern: face recognised, but the emotional ‘feeling of familiarity’ is missing.</li><li>Social skills training for people with schizophrenia, using modelling, role-play, feedback and homework, is most closely associated with:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This is an actual previous-year question (IHBAS 2024). Examiners do repeat questions, sometimes word for word, and this area also appeared in GMCH 2023, JEMAS 2022. Make sure you can answer it instantly — and keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 38, "question": "Social skills training for people with schizophrenia, using modelling, role-play, feedback and homework, is most closely associated with:", "options": ["Fritz Perls", "Aaron Beck", "Robert Paul Liberman", "Salvador Minuchin"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Robert Liberman (UCLA) developed structured social and independent living skills modules (e.g. medication and symptom management) for people with serious mental illness.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Beck developed cognitive therapy, later adapted for psychosis.</li><li>Minuchin developed structural family therapy.</li><li>Perls developed Gestalt therapy.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Skills training is a key part of psychiatric rehabilitation.</li><li>Generalisation to real life is the main challenge.</li><li>Bellack and Mueser wrote a widely used social skills manual.</li><li>When an individual with multiple cognitive disabilities has extraordinary proficiency in one isolated skill, this is known as:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2024, Gurugram. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 39, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · RINPAS 2024</span>When an individual with multiple cognitive disabilities has extraordinary proficiency in one isolated skill, this is known as:", "options": ["Rainman syndrome", "Asperger ability", "Intellectual isolation", "Savant syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Savant syndrome is a striking ‘island of genius’ (calendar calculation, music, art, memory) in a person with a developmental disability, most often autism.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Rainman syndrome’ is a popular nickname after the 1988 film, not the clinical term.</li><li>‘Asperger ability’ is not a recognised term.</li><li>‘Intellectual isolation’ is not a clinical term.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>About half of people with savant syndrome are autistic (Treffert).</li><li>J. Langdon Down used the term ‘idiot savant’ in 1887; it is now outdated.</li><li>Splinter skills are smaller areas of unusual ability.</li><li>In A.R. Luria’s model of the brain’s three functional units, the unit for programming, regulating and verifying activity is located in the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This is an actual previous-year question (RINPAS 2024). Examiners do repeat questions, sometimes word for word. Make sure you can answer it instantly — and keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 40, "question": "In A.R. Luria’s model of the brain’s three functional units, the unit for programming, regulating and verifying activity is located in the:", "options": ["Brainstem and reticular formation", "Frontal lobes", "Occipital, temporal and parietal lobes", "Cerebellum"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Luria (The Working Brain, 1973) described: Unit 1 – regulating tone and wakefulness (brainstem, reticular formation); Unit 2 – receiving, analysing and storing information (posterior cortex); Unit 3 – programming, regulating and verifying activity (frontal lobes).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The brainstem and reticular formation form Unit 1 (arousal).</li><li>The posterior lobes form Unit 2 (information processing).</li><li>The cerebellum is not one of Luria’s three units.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Luria’s qualitative approach led to the Luria–Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery.</li><li>Luria also studied a mnemonist (S.) in ‘The Mind of a Mnemonist’.</li><li>Each unit has primary, secondary and tertiary zones.</li><li>Russell Barkley’s theory proposes that the core deficit in ADHD is a problem with:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in M.Phil Entrance 2021. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 41, "question": "Russell Barkley’s theory proposes that the core deficit in ADHD is a problem with:", "options": ["Phonological processing", "Theory of mind", "Long-term memory storage", "Behavioural inhibition"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Barkley (1997) argued that poor behavioural inhibition is primary in ADHD. It then disrupts executive functions such as working memory, self-regulation of emotion, inner speech and planning.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Theory of mind deficits are linked to autism.</li><li>Phonological processing deficits are linked to dyslexia.</li><li>Long-term memory storage is not the core problem.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Sonuga-Barke’s dual-pathway model adds delay aversion.</li><li>Barkley also developed parent training programmes for defiant children.</li><li>ADHD is one of the most heritable psychiatric disorders (about 70–80%).</li><li>Before a seizure, Rahul smells burning rubber and feels he has lived the moment before. During it he smacks his lips and fumbles with his shirt, and later he remembers nothing. The seizure most likely starts in the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 42, "question": "Before a seizure, Rahul smells burning rubber and feels he has lived the moment before. During it he smacks his lips and fumbles with his shirt, and later he remembers nothing. The seizure most likely starts in the:", "options": ["Cerebellum", "Parietal lobe", "Temporal lobe", "Occipital lobe"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> An olfactory aura, déjà vu, automatisms such as lip-smacking, and impaired awareness are typical of temporal lobe (focal impaired awareness) seizures, often from the hippocampus and amygdala.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Occipital seizures give flashing lights or visual changes.</li><li>Parietal seizures give tingling or other bodily sensations.</li><li>The cerebellum does not generate such seizures.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Temporal lobe epilepsy is the most common focal epilepsy in adults.</li><li>Déjà vu and jamais vu are disturbances of familiarity.</li><li>H.M.’s surgery was done to treat his epilepsy.</li><li>The Modified Checklist for Autism in Toddlers (M-CHAT) is designed for screening children aged about:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RML 2024, RINPAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 43, "question": "The Modified Checklist for Autism in Toddlers (M-CHAT) is designed for screening children aged about:", "options": ["16 to 30 months", "3 to 6 years", "6 to 12 months", "6 to 18 years"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The M-CHAT (Robins and colleagues, 2001; revised as M-CHAT-R/F) is a parent-report screen for toddlers aged 16–30 months. It asks about joint attention, pointing, pretend play and response to name.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>3–6 years is too late for this toddler tool.</li><li>6–12 months is too early; many items cannot yet be judged.</li><li>6–18 years is the range of other tools.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Lack of joint attention (pointing to share interest) is one of the earliest signs of autism.</li><li>ADOS-2 is the standard observational assessment.</li><li>ISAA is the Indian tool for autism assessment and disability certification.</li><li>After a head injury, Mr. Verma’s memory has not recovered. The psychologist trains him to use a diary, phone reminders and labelled cupboards instead of trying to restore his memory. This approach is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 44, "question": "After a head injury, Mr. Verma’s memory has not recovered. The psychologist trains him to use a diary, phone reminders and labelled cupboards instead of trying to restore his memory. This approach is:", "options": ["Restorative rehabilitation", "Compensatory rehabilitation", "Behavioural activation", "Psychodynamic therapy"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Cognitive rehabilitation is either restorative (retraining the impaired function through drills) or compensatory (finding other ways, using external aids or environmental changes, to get around the deficit).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Restorative methods try to repair the function itself through practice.</li><li>Psychodynamic therapy explores unconscious conflicts.</li><li>Behavioural activation treats depression by scheduling rewarding activities.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>External memory aids are among the best-supported memory interventions.</li><li>NeuroPage (a paging reminder system) was studied by Wilson and colleagues.</li><li>GMCH 2023 asked what the main aim of psychological rehabilitation is.</li><li>In the Rey–Osterrieth Complex Figure Test, the person first copies a complex drawing and later draws it from memory. The two parts mainly assess:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GMCH 2023, JEMAS 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 45, "question": "In the Rey–Osterrieth Complex Figure Test, the person first copies a complex drawing and later draws it from memory. The two parts mainly assess:", "options": ["Motor speed and hand–eye coordination", "Abstract reasoning and set-shifting", "Verbal learning and verbal recognition", "Visuoconstruction and visual memory"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The copy trial tests visuoconstructional ability and organisation (planning); the delayed recall trial tests non-verbal (visual) memory.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Verbal learning is tested by lists such as the RAVLT.</li><li>Motor speed is tested by finger tapping.</li><li>Set-shifting is tested by the WCST.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>André Rey (1941) devised it; Paul Osterrieth (1944) standardised it.</li><li>A piecemeal copying style suggests poor executive organisation.</li><li>The BVRT is another visual memory test.</li><li>In autistic spectrum disorder, when an individual exhibits immediate imitation of words or sounds they have just heard, this is known as:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IMHANS 2025. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 46, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · RINPAS 2024</span>In autistic spectrum disorder, when an individual exhibits immediate imitation of words or sounds they have just heard, this is known as:", "options": ["Echoastic disorder", "Phonological inhibition", "Echolalia", "Grapheme dysfunction"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Echolalia is the repetition of words or phrases spoken by others. It can be immediate (right after hearing) or delayed (hours or days later, e.g. repeating TV lines). It is common in autism.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Echoastic disorder’ is not a real term.</li><li>Phonological inhibition is not a clinical term for this behaviour.</li><li>Grapheme dysfunction would relate to written letters, not spoken repetition.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Echopraxia is the imitation of another person’s movements.</li><li>Echo phenomena are also seen in catatonia and Tourette’s disorder.</li><li>Pronoun reversal (saying ‘you’ for ‘I’) is another classic autistic speech feature.</li><li>Shown a drawing of a key, Mrs. Paul can copy it accurately, but she cannot say what it is or what it is used for. When the key is placed in her hand, she names it at once. This is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This is an actual previous-year question (RINPAS 2024). Examiners do repeat questions, sometimes word for word, and this area also appeared in JEPAS 2020. Make sure you can answer it instantly — and keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 47, "question": "Shown a drawing of a key, Mrs. Paul can copy it accurately, but she cannot say what it is or what it is used for. When the key is placed in her hand, she names it at once. This is:", "options": ["Associative visual agnosia", "Optic ataxia", "Apperceptive visual agnosia", "Anomic aphasia"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In associative agnosia, perception is intact (she can copy), but the percept cannot be linked to stored meaning. Naming by touch shows that her language and knowledge of the object are intact.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>In apperceptive agnosia, perception itself fails, so copying is poor.</li><li>In anomic aphasia, naming fails in all senses, including touch.</li><li>Optic ataxia is poor visually guided reaching.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Lissauer (1890) first divided visual agnosias into apperceptive and associative.</li><li>Associative agnosia usually follows bilateral occipito-temporal damage.</li><li>Prosopagnosia is a face-specific visual agnosia.</li><li>‘Heller’s syndrome’ (dementia infantilis) is the older name for:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 48, "question": "‘Heller’s syndrome’ (dementia infantilis) is the older name for:", "options": ["Asperger’s disorder", "Childhood disintegrative disorder", "Landau–Kleffner syndrome", "Rett syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Theodor Heller (1908) described children who developed normally for at least 2 years and then lost language, social, play and toilet skills. DSM-IV called it childhood disintegrative disorder; DSM-5 includes it under autism spectrum disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Rett syndrome was described by Andreas Rett (1966).</li><li>Asperger’s disorder had no language delay or regression.</li><li>Landau–Kleffner syndrome is acquired aphasia with epilepsy.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RINPAS 2024 listed CDD among the conditions grouped under ASD.</li><li>DSM-IV’s pervasive developmental disorders: autistic, Rett’s, CDD, Asperger’s, PDD-NOS.</li><li>Regression after normal development needs a neurological work-up.</li><li>Damage to the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex most typically causes:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RINPAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 49, "question": "Damage to the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex most typically causes:", "options": ["Poor planning, rigid thinking and perseveration", "Apathy with little speech or movement (abulia)", "Disinhibited, tactless and impulsive social behaviour", "Loss of the ability to recognise familiar faces"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex supports executive functions — planning, working memory, set-shifting and abstract thinking. Damage leads to a ‘dysexecutive’ picture with perseveration on tests such as the WCST.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Disinhibition is typical of orbitofrontal damage (as in Phineas Gage).</li><li>Apathy and abulia are typical of medial frontal / anterior cingulate damage.</li><li>Face recognition problems follow fusiform (occipito-temporal) damage.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Three frontal syndromes: dorsolateral (dysexecutive), orbitofrontal (disinhibited), medial (apathetic).</li><li>NFSU 2023 and RINPAS 2024 asked which lobe damage makes a person impulsive and irresponsible.</li><li>Frontal tests: WCST, Stroop, verbal fluency, Trail Making B.</li><li>The Developmental Screening Test (DST), an Indian tool that estimates developmental age from birth to 15 years, was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, GBU 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 50, "question": "The Developmental Screening Test (DST), an Indian tool that estimates developmental age from birth to 15 years, was developed by:", "options": ["A.J. Malin", "C.M. Bhatia", "J. Bharat Raj", "V.V. Kamat"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> J. Bharat Raj (1983) developed the DST, with 88 items on motor, social and language milestones. It gives a developmental age and developmental quotient.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Malin adapted the Vineland Social Maturity Scale and the WISC (MISIC).</li><li>Kamat adapted the Binet scale.</li><li>Bhatia developed a battery of performance tests of intelligence.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Developmental quotient (DQ) = developmental age ÷ chronological age × 100.</li><li>The DST is often used with the VSMS for disability certification.</li><li>Gesell developed early developmental schedules.</li><li>After a stroke, Mr. Joseph talks fast and fluently, but his speech is full of wrong and made-up words. He cannot follow simple commands and seems unaware that he makes no sense. The lesion is most likely in the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RML 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 51, "question": "After a stroke, Mr. Joseph talks fast and fluently, but his speech is full of wrong and made-up words. He cannot follow simple commands and seems unaware that he makes no sense. The lesion is most likely in the:", "options": ["Left arcuate fasciculus", "Left posterior superior temporal gyrus (Wernicke’s area)", "Right posterior superior temporal gyrus", "Left inferior frontal gyrus (Broca’s area)"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Fluent, meaningless speech with neologisms and paraphasias, poor comprehension and poor awareness of errors is Wernicke’s (receptive) aphasia, from damage to the left posterior superior temporal gyrus (Brodmann area 22).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Broca’s area damage gives slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with fairly good comprehension.</li><li>Arcuate fasciculus damage gives conduction aphasia — good comprehension, poor repetition.</li><li>The right temporal lobe does not usually control language in right-handers.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Wernicke described sensory aphasia in 1874.</li><li>Fluent speech with neologisms is sometimes called ‘word salad’ or jargon aphasia.</li><li>JANAS asked what damage to Wernicke’s area causes.</li><li>In discrete trial training, the basic teaching unit follows the sequence:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024, GMCH 2023 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 52, "question": "In discrete trial training, the basic teaching unit follows the sequence:", "options": ["Consequence → instruction → response", "Free play → interpretation → insight", "Modelling by peers → imitation → self-evaluation", "Instruction (discriminative stimulus) → child’s response → consequence"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Each discrete trial has a clear start and end: the therapist gives an instruction (SD), sometimes with a prompt, the child responds, and a consequence follows (reinforcement or correction), then a short pause.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The consequence comes after the response, not first.</li><li>Peer modelling and self-evaluation describe social learning, not DTT.</li><li>Interpretation and insight belong to psychodynamic therapy.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>This is the three-term contingency (A-B-C) of operant learning.</li><li>Prompts are faded gradually so the child responds on their own.</li><li>DTT is criticised for poor generalisation to natural settings.</li><li>Which change did DSM-5 make about ADHD and autism spectrum disorder?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 53, "question": "Which change did DSM-5 make about ADHD and autism spectrum disorder?", "options": ["Both can now be diagnosed together in the same person", "ADHD cannot be diagnosed if autism is present", "Autism is now diagnosed only after ADHD is ruled out", "ADHD is now a subtype of autism spectrum disorder"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-IV did not allow ADHD to be diagnosed during a pervasive developmental disorder. DSM-5 removed this rule, so ADHD and ASD can be diagnosed together — which is common in practice.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ADHD is a separate neurodevelopmental disorder, not part of ASD.</li><li>This was the old DSM-IV exclusion rule.</li><li>There is no such ordering rule.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 moved ADHD to the new neurodevelopmental disorders chapter.</li><li>DSM-5 raised the age-of-onset rule from 7 to 12 years.</li><li>Adults (17+) need 5 rather than 6 symptoms.</li><li>Short, personalised stories that describe a social situation, the cues in it and the expected responses, to help autistic children understand social rules, were developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 54, "question": "Short, personalised stories that describe a social situation, the cues in it and the expected responses, to help autistic children understand social rules, were developed by:", "options": ["Lorna Wing", "Simon Baron-Cohen", "Eric Schopler", "Carol Gray"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Carol Gray introduced Social Stories in 1991. They are written from the child’s view, using descriptive, perspective and directive sentences.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Schopler developed TEACCH.</li><li>Wing introduced the ‘triad of impairments’.</li><li>Baron-Cohen proposed the theory-of-mind account of autism.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Comic Strip Conversations are another of Gray’s tools.</li><li>Video modelling is a related visual strategy.</li><li>Social skills groups give practice with peers.</li><li>In DSM-5, ‘global developmental delay’ is diagnosed when:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 55, "question": "In DSM-5, ‘global developmental delay’ is diagnosed when:", "options": ["Any child has an IQ below 70 with deficits in adaptive behaviour", "A child shows delay in one area only, such as speech", "A child under 5 years fails to meet several developmental milestones and cannot yet be reliably assessed for intellectual disability", "A child over 10 years has lost previously acquired language and social skills"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Global developmental delay is a temporary diagnosis for children under 5 whose severity cannot be reliably judged with standard testing. They should be reassessed later.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IQ below 70 with adaptive deficits describes intellectual disability.</li><li>Loss of skills (regression) suggests other conditions.</li><li>Delay in one area only is not ‘global’.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Unspecified intellectual disability’ is for children over 5 who cannot be assessed (e.g. due to blindness).</li><li>Developmental quotients estimate the level of delay.</li><li>Early intervention is advised once delay is found.</li><li>In a dichotic listening task, different words are played to each ear at the same time. Most right-handed people report more words heard by the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RML 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 56, "question": "In a dichotic listening task, different words are played to each ear at the same time. Most right-handed people report more words heard by the:", "options": ["Left ear, because it sends its strongest input to the left hemisphere", "Right ear, because it sends its strongest input to the left hemisphere", "Left ear, because the right hemisphere is dominant for words", "Right ear, because each ear connects only to the hemisphere on its own side"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Each ear sends its stronger (contralateral) pathway to the opposite hemisphere. Words from the right ear reach the left, language-dominant hemisphere directly, producing a ‘right-ear advantage’ for verbal material (Doreen Kimura, 1961).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The left ear’s main pathway goes to the right hemisphere.</li><li>The main auditory pathways cross; they are not only same-sided.</li><li>In most people, the left hemisphere, not the right, is dominant for words.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>For music and environmental sounds a left-ear advantage is often seen.</li><li>Dichotic listening is a non-invasive way to estimate lateralisation.</li><li>Broadbent used dichotic listening to study selective attention.</li><li>Eight-year-old Kunal is very clumsy. He often drops things, has poor handwriting and cannot ride a bicycle or catch a ball, unlike his classmates. His intelligence and vision are normal. The most likely diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this exact point has not been asked directly yet, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics. It may come in your exam in a new form — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 57, "question": "Eight-year-old Kunal is very clumsy. He often drops things, has poor handwriting and cannot ride a bicycle or catch a ball, unlike his classmates. His intelligence and vision are normal. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Developmental coordination disorder", "Cerebral palsy", "Specific learning disorder with impairment in written expression", "Stereotypic movement disorder"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Developmental coordination disorder (often called dyspraxia) is motor skill well below age expectation, causing problems in daily life and school, not explained by a neurological condition, ID or vision problems.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>His problem is motor, not written expression in spelling, grammar or organisation.</li><li>Cerebral palsy involves a neurological lesion with abnormal muscle tone.</li><li>Stereotypic movements are repetitive, purposeless movements such as rocking.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DCD, stereotypic movement disorder and tic disorders are the ‘motor disorders’ in DSM-5.</li><li>DCD often co-occurs with ADHD.</li><li>Occupational therapy is a main intervention.</li><li>After a right frontal stroke, Mrs. Iyer understands speech well, but her own speech sounds flat and robot-like. She cannot make her voice sound happy, angry or sad even when asked. This is best called:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, RINPAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 58, "question": "After a right frontal stroke, Mrs. Iyer understands speech well, but her own speech sounds flat and robot-like. She cannot make her voice sound happy, angry or sad even when asked. This is best called:", "options": ["Broca’s aphasia", "Dysarthria due to cerebellar damage", "Motor aprosodia", "Alexithymia"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Prosody (the melody and emotional tone of speech) depends mainly on the right hemisphere. Elliott Ross (1981) called loss of expressive prosody after right frontal damage ‘motor aprosodia’ — a right-hemisphere mirror of Broca’s aphasia.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Broca’s aphasia affects word production and grammar, and follows left-sided damage.</li><li>Cerebellar dysarthria gives slurred, scanning speech, not loss of emotional tone.</li><li>Alexithymia is difficulty identifying feelings, not a speech problem.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Sensory aprosodia (right temporo-parietal damage) is difficulty understanding emotional tone.</li><li>The right hemisphere is also stronger for face recognition and spatial attention.</li><li>Flat prosody is also seen in depression, schizophrenia and Parkinson’s disease.</li><li>Which drug is a non-stimulant medicine approved for ADHD, acting mainly by blocking noradrenaline reuptake?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, JANAS. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 59, "question": "Which drug is a non-stimulant medicine approved for ADHD, acting mainly by blocking noradrenaline reuptake?", "options": ["Dextroamphetamine", "Risperidone", "Methylphenidate", "Atomoxetine"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Atomoxetine is a selective noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor. It is useful when stimulants are not tolerated, or when there is a risk of misuse or tics.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Methylphenidate is a stimulant that blocks dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake.</li><li>Dextroamphetamine is a stimulant.</li><li>Risperidone is an antipsychotic used for irritability in autism, not core ADHD.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Stimulants are first-line in most guidelines.</li><li>Guanfacine and clonidine (alpha-2 agonists) are other non-stimulants.</li><li>Behavioural parent training is first-line for preschool children.</li><li>A patient with damage to the primary visual cortex says he sees nothing in part of his visual field, yet he can point to objects there far better than chance. This phenomenon is called:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 60, "question": "A patient with damage to the primary visual cortex says he sees nothing in part of his visual field, yet he can point to objects there far better than chance. This phenomenon is called:", "options": ["Charles Bonnet syndrome", "Blindsight", "Hemispatial neglect", "Anton’s syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Blindsight (studied by Lawrence Weiskrantz in the 1970s) is the ability to respond to visual stimuli in a ‘blind’ field without conscious seeing. It is thought to use pathways that bypass V1, such as the superior colliculus.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Anton’s syndrome is the opposite: the person is blind but claims to see.</li><li>In neglect, the person ignores one side but the visual cortex may be intact.</li><li>Charles Bonnet syndrome involves visual hallucinations in visually impaired people.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Blindsight is used in debates on consciousness.</li><li>RINPAS 2024 listed blindsight as an option with aphasia and apraxia.</li><li>V1 = Brodmann area 17 (striate cortex).</li><li>A child with autism is given a sticker for every 10 minutes that pass without head-banging, regardless of what else he does. This procedure is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RINPAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 61, "question": "A child with autism is given a sticker for every 10 minutes that pass without head-banging, regardless of what else he does. This procedure is:", "options": ["Differential reinforcement of incompatible behaviour (DRI)", "Extinction", "Differential reinforcement of other behaviour (DRO)", "Differential reinforcement of alternative behaviour (DRA)"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In DRO, reinforcement is given for the absence of the problem behaviour for a set period. In DRI, a behaviour that cannot occur at the same time (e.g. hands on lap) is reinforced; in DRA, a specific alternative behaviour is reinforced.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DRI would reward a specific incompatible behaviour, such as keeping hands in the lap.</li><li>DRA would reward a specific alternative, such as asking for help.</li><li>Extinction withholds the reinforcer that maintains the behaviour.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DRL (low rates) rewards a behaviour that happens less often.</li><li>Differential reinforcement is preferred over punishment.</li><li>A functional assessment helps choose the right procedure.</li><li>The most common inherited (single-gene) cause of intellectual disability is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 62, "question": "The most common inherited (single-gene) cause of intellectual disability is:", "options": ["Fetal alcohol syndrome", "Phenylketonuria", "Down syndrome", "Fragile X syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Fragile X syndrome is caused by an expanded CGG repeat in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome. It is the most common inherited cause of ID; boys are more severely affected. Features include a long face, large ears and autistic traits.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Down syndrome is the most common genetic (chromosomal) cause, but it is usually not inherited.</li><li>PKU is inherited but much rarer and preventable with diet.</li><li>Fetal alcohol syndrome is not genetic.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>More than 200 CGG repeats = full mutation.</li><li>Many boys with fragile X also meet criteria for autism.</li><li>Large testes after puberty (macro-orchidism) is a classic sign.</li><li>A: When parents stop giving attention to a child’s tantrums, the tantrums may first become worse before they decrease.  R: A behaviour that is no longer reinforced stops immediately.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2025, RML 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 63, "question": "<p class=\"stem-lead\">Read the Assertion (A) and Reason (R) and choose the correct option:</p><div class=\"ar-block\"><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Assertion (A)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">When parents stop giving attention to a child’s tantrums, the tantrums may first become worse before they decrease.</span></div><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Reason (R)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">A behaviour that is no longer reinforced stops immediately.</span></div></div>", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A is true: this is the ‘extinction burst’ — a short rise in the frequency or intensity of a behaviour when reinforcement stops. R is false: extinction is gradual, and the behaviour can even return later (spontaneous recovery).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A and B are wrong because R is false.</li><li>D is wrong because A is true.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Parents must stay consistent during the burst, or they teach the child that stronger tantrums work.</li><li>Behaviours on intermittent reinforcement resist extinction more.</li><li>Planned ignoring is used in parent management training.</li><li>Klüver–Bucy syndrome, which follows bilateral damage to the anterior temporal lobes, includes all of the following, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 64, "question": "Klüver–Bucy syndrome, which follows bilateral damage to the anterior temporal lobes, includes all of the following, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Increased fear and aggression", "Hyperorality (putting objects in the mouth)", "Visual agnosia (‘psychic blindness’)", "Hypersexuality"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Klüver and Bucy (1937) removed both temporal lobes in monkeys and saw tameness (placidity), hyperorality, hypersexuality and visual agnosia. Loss of the amygdala reduces fear, so the animals became calm, not more fearful or aggressive.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Hyperorality is a core feature.</li><li>Hypersexuality is a core feature.</li><li>Visual agnosia (‘psychic blindness’) is a core feature.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>In humans, causes include herpes simplex encephalitis and frontotemporal dementia.</li><li>The amygdala is central to fear and emotional learning.</li><li>JEMAS 2024 asked what Klüver–Bucy syndrome is characterised by.</li><li>Autism spectrum disorder is diagnosed in boys and girls in a ratio of roughly:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IMHANS 2025, JEMAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 65, "question": "Autism spectrum disorder is diagnosed in boys and girls in a ratio of roughly:", "options": ["1 : 1", "4 : 1", "10 : 1", "1 : 4"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5-TR gives a male-to-female ratio of about 3–4 : 1. Girls may be under-diagnosed because they ‘camouflage’ social difficulties better.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>1 : 1 is not correct; boys are diagnosed more often.</li><li>1 : 4 reverses the ratio.</li><li>10 : 1 is too high.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Boys are also diagnosed more often with ADHD.</li><li>Rett syndrome is almost only seen in girls.</li><li>Autism prevalence is about 1% worldwide.</li><li>The NIMHANS Neuropsychological Battery (2004), widely used in India, was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 66, "question": "The NIMHANS Neuropsychological Battery (2004), widely used in India, was developed by:", "options": ["Shobini L. Rao and colleagues", "Dwarka Pershad and S.K. Verma", "V.V. Kamat", "A.J. Malin"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Shobini L. Rao, D.K. Subbakrishna and K. Gopukumar published the NIMHANS Neuropsychology Battery (2004), with Indian norms for tests of attention, executive function, memory and visuospatial skills.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Pershad and Verma developed the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction.</li><li>Kamat adapted the Binet test (Binet-Kamat Test).</li><li>Malin adapted the WISC (MISIC) and the VSMS.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The battery has separate norms for adults and children/adolescents.</li><li>It includes Indian versions of Stroop, Tower of London, verbal fluency and the AVLT.</li><li>Indian tools are a regular question area in IHBAS and JEMAS papers.</li><li>The discredited ‘refrigerator mother’ theory, which blamed cold, distant parenting for autism, was popularised mainly by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GBU 2025, IHBAS 2024, RML 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 67, "question": "The discredited ‘refrigerator mother’ theory, which blamed cold, distant parenting for autism, was popularised mainly by:", "options": ["Michael Rutter", "Lorna Wing", "Bernard Rimland", "Bruno Bettelheim"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Kanner first hinted at it, but Bruno Bettelheim popularised the idea in ‘The Empty Fortress’ (1967). Research has firmly rejected it; autism is a neurodevelopmental condition with strong genetic influence.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Bernard Rimland argued against the theory (Infantile Autism, 1964).</li><li>Michael Rutter’s twin studies showed genetic influence.</li><li>Lorna Wing introduced the ‘triad of impairments’ and the term ‘Asperger syndrome’.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Folstein and Rutter (1977) did the first autism twin study.</li><li>Autism heritability is estimated at about 80%.</li><li>GMCH 2023 asked about the vaccine–autism hypothesis in a research-design question.</li><li>Mr. Sharma, 58, has become rude, tactless and careless with money, and he overeats sweets. He shows little concern about these changes. His memory and orientation are still fairly good. The most likely diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 68, "question": "Mr. Sharma, 58, has become rude, tactless and careless with money, and he overeats sweets. He shows little concern about these changes. His memory and orientation are still fairly good. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Dementia with Lewy bodies", "Frontotemporal dementia (behavioural variant)", "Alzheimer’s disease", "Vascular dementia"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Early personality change, disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy and changes in eating, with relatively preserved memory, point to behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia (once called Pick’s disease). Onset is often before 65.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Alzheimer’s disease usually begins with memory loss.</li><li>Lewy body dementia shows fluctuating attention, visual hallucinations and parkinsonism.</li><li>Vascular dementia shows a stepwise course with focal neurological signs.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Arnold Pick described the condition in 1892.</li><li>Klüver–Bucy-like features (hyperorality) can appear in FTD.</li><li>Semantic dementia is a language variant of FTD.</li><li>In memory rehabilitation for amnesic patients, Baddeley and Wilson (1994) recommended ‘errorless learning’ because:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2025, M.Phil Entrance 2021, JEPAS 2020. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 69, "question": "In memory rehabilitation for amnesic patients, Baddeley and Wilson (1994) recommended ‘errorless learning’ because:", "options": ["Patients with poor explicit memory cannot remember and correct their own mistakes, so errors get strengthened", "It restores the damaged hippocampus to normal", "Making errors improves later recall in amnesic patients", "It works mainly by improving motor speed"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In errorless learning, the patient is prevented from guessing wrongly (e.g. by giving the answer first). People with amnesia rely on implicit memory, which strengthens whatever response was made, including mistakes.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>In healthy people trial-and-error can help, but not in amnesia.</li><li>It does not restore damaged tissue; it works around it.</li><li>It is a memory method, not a motor speed method.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Vanishing cues and spaced retrieval are other memory rehabilitation methods.</li><li>Barbara Wilson set up the Oliver Zangwill Centre for neuropsychological rehabilitation.</li><li>Errorless learning is also used in dementia care.</li><li>The PGI Memory Scale (1977), an Indian test of memory functions, was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 70, "question": "The PGI Memory Scale (1977), an Indian test of memory functions, was developed by:", "options": ["Shobini Rao and D.K. Subbakrishna", "Dwarka Pershad and N.N. Wig", "Uma Chowdhury", "C.M. Bhatia"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Dwarka Pershad and N.N. Wig developed the PGI Memory Scale at PGIMER, Chandigarh. It has 10 subtests covering remote and recent memory, mental balance, attention, immediate recall, retention and recognition.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Rao and Subbakrishna are linked with the NIMHANS battery.</li><li>Bhatia made the Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence.</li><li>Uma Chowdhury adapted the TAT for India.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The PGI Memory Scale is part of the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction (PGI-BBD).</li><li>It is modelled partly on the Wechsler Memory Scale.</li><li>The WMS was developed by David Wechsler (1945).</li><li>TEACCH, a structured-teaching programme for autism that uses visual schedules and an organised physical environment, was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this exact point has not been asked directly yet, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics. It may come in your exam in a new form — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 71, "question": "TEACCH, a structured-teaching programme for autism that uses visual schedules and an organised physical environment, was developed by:", "options": ["Andy Bondy and Lori Frost", "Carol Gray", "Eric Schopler", "O. Ivar Lovaas"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Eric Schopler started TEACCH (Treatment and Education of Autistic and related Communication-handicapped Children) at the University of North Carolina in the early 1970s. It builds on the visual strengths of autistic people.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Lovaas is linked with intensive ABA.</li><li>Bondy and Frost developed PECS.</li><li>Carol Gray developed Social Stories.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Schopler also co-developed the Childhood Autism Rating Scale (CARS).</li><li>TEACCH involves parents as co-therapists.</li><li>Work systems show the child what to do, how much, and what comes next.</li><li>The lesions most consistently found in Korsakoff syndrome involve the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 72, "question": "The lesions most consistently found in Korsakoff syndrome involve the:", "options": ["Prefrontal cortex and corpus callosum", "Basal ganglia and substantia nigra", "Hippocampus and amygdala only", "Mammillary bodies and medial (dorsomedial) thalamus"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Korsakoff syndrome follows thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, usually in chronic alcohol use. Damage to the mammillary bodies and dorsomedial thalamus causes dense anterograde amnesia, retrograde amnesia and confabulation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The hippocampus is involved in other amnesias (e.g. H.M.), but not most consistently here.</li><li>Basal ganglia and substantia nigra are affected in Parkinson’s disease.</li><li>Frontal involvement may add to confabulation, but it is not the core lesion.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Wernicke’s encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia) often comes first.</li><li>Immediate memory (digit span) is usually preserved.</li><li>JEMAS 2024 and RML 2024 asked which illness causes profound retrograde amnesia (Korsakoff’s).</li><li>Riya’s mother tells her, ‘You can watch cartoons after you finish your homework.’ This uses a more preferred activity to reinforce a less preferred one, known as the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as RML 2024, JEMAS 2024, IHBAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 73, "question": "Riya’s mother tells her, ‘You can watch cartoons after you finish your homework.’ This uses a more preferred activity to reinforce a less preferred one, known as the:", "options": ["Zeigarnik effect", "Yerkes–Dodson law", "Law of effect", "Premack principle"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> David Premack (1959) showed that a high-probability behaviour can reinforce a low-probability behaviour. It is also called ‘Grandma’s rule’.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The Yerkes–Dodson law links arousal and performance.</li><li>Thorndike’s law of effect says satisfying outcomes strengthen behaviour, but it is not this specific principle.</li><li>The Zeigarnik effect is better memory for unfinished tasks.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Premack’s principle shows reinforcers are relative, not fixed.</li><li>It is used in classrooms and parent training.</li><li>Response deprivation theory later refined it.</li><li>Broca’s area corresponds mainly to which Brodmann areas?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 74, "question": "Broca’s area corresponds mainly to which Brodmann areas?", "options": ["41 and 42", "1, 2 and 3", "44 and 45", "17 and 18"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Broca’s area lies in the left inferior frontal gyrus (pars opercularis and pars triangularis), Brodmann areas 44 and 45.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>41 and 42 are the primary auditory cortex.</li><li>17 is primary visual cortex; 18 is visual association cortex.</li><li>1, 2 and 3 form the primary somatosensory cortex.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Wernicke’s area is mainly Brodmann area 22.</li><li>Primary motor cortex = area 4.</li><li>Korbinian Brodmann mapped 52 areas in 1909.</li><li>Aarav, a 5-year-old non-verbal autistic boy, gives his teacher a card with a picture of a biscuit and receives a biscuit. This communication method is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 75, "question": "Aarav, a 5-year-old non-verbal autistic boy, gives his teacher a card with a picture of a biscuit and receives a biscuit. This communication method is:", "options": ["Picture Exchange Communication System (PECS)", "Social Stories", "Facilitated communication", "Sign language"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> PECS (Bondy and Frost, 1985) teaches the child to start communication by handing a picture to a partner in exchange for the wanted item. It moves in phases from single pictures to sentence strips.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Sign language uses hand signs, not picture cards.</li><li>Social Stories are short stories explaining social situations.</li><li>Facilitated communication involves someone guiding the person’s hand, and is discredited.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>PECS is a form of augmentative and alternative communication (AAC).</li><li>PECS does not stop speech from developing; it may help it.</li><li>Its six phases end with commenting.</li><li>Match the test (List I) with the function it mainly assesses (List II):</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 76, "question": "Match the test (List I) with the function it mainly assesses (List II): Codes:<div class=\"mt-wrap\"><table class=\"match-table\"><thead><tr><th>List I</th><th>List II</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Tower of London</td><td>a. Motor speed</td></tr><tr><td>2. Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test</td><td>b. Visuospatial perception</td></tr><tr><td>3. Benton Judgement of Line Orientation</td><td>c. Planning and problem-solving</td></tr><tr><td>4. Finger Tapping Test</td><td>d. Verbal learning and memory</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "options": ["1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a", "1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a", "1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b", "1-c, 2-b, 3-d, 4-a"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Tower of London (Shallice, 1982) – planning; RAVLT (Rey, 1964) – learning a 15-word list over trials; Judgement of Line Orientation (Benton) – visuospatial perception; Finger Tapping (Halstead–Reitan battery) – fine motor speed.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The other codes swap planning with verbal memory, or visuospatial perception with motor speed.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Tower of Hanoi is a similar planning task.</li><li>Digit span forwards tests attention span; backwards tests working memory.</li><li>Clock drawing tests visuoconstruction and planning.</li><li>A man who wants to stop drinking is asked to imagine, in vivid detail, feeling sick and vomiting as he lifts a glass of liquor. This technique is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IMHANS 2025, JEPAS 2020, MP ENT. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 77, "question": "A man who wants to stop drinking is asked to imagine, in vivid detail, feeling sick and vomiting as he lifts a glass of liquor. This technique is:", "options": ["Covert modelling", "Covert sensitisation", "Systematic desensitisation", "Flooding"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Covert sensitisation (Joseph Cautela, 1967) pairs the imagined unwanted behaviour with imagined unpleasant consequences, all in imagination, to reduce the urge.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Systematic desensitisation reduces fear using relaxation and a graded hierarchy.</li><li>Flooding is prolonged exposure to a feared stimulus.</li><li>Covert modelling means imagining someone else doing the desired behaviour.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Aversion therapy uses real unpleasant stimuli, such as a nausea-producing drug.</li><li>Disulfiram works on a similar aversive principle for alcohol.</li><li>Cautela also described covert reinforcement.</li><li>After a stroke damaging both occipital lobes, Mrs. Das walks into furniture but insists she can see perfectly and describes the room in made-up detail. This is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024, IHBAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 78, "question": "After a stroke damaging both occipital lobes, Mrs. Das walks into furniture but insists she can see perfectly and describes the room in made-up detail. This is:", "options": ["Charles Bonnet syndrome", "Balint’s syndrome", "Blindsight", "Anton’s syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Anton’s syndrome is cortical blindness from bilateral occipital damage combined with denial of the blindness (anosognosia), often with confabulation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Charles Bonnet syndrome is vivid visual hallucinations with intact insight in visually impaired people.</li><li>Balint’s syndrome involves simultanagnosia and optic ataxia, not denial.</li><li>Blindsight is the reverse: the person says she cannot see, yet responds to visual stimuli.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Anton’s syndrome is also called Anton–Babinski syndrome.</li><li>It usually follows posterior cerebral artery strokes.</li><li>Confabulation is also seen in Korsakoff syndrome.</li><li>Family interventions for schizophrenia reduce relapse mainly by lowering the family’s:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 79, "question": "Family interventions for schizophrenia reduce relapse mainly by lowering the family’s:", "options": ["Use of the patient’s mother tongue at home", "Socio-economic status", "Expressed emotion — critical comments, hostility and emotional over-involvement", "Genetic risk for schizophrenia"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> High expressed emotion (EE) in relatives predicts relapse (Brown, Birley and Wing, 1972; Vaughn and Leff, 1976). Psychoeducation, communication and problem-solving training reduce EE and relapse.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Income is not what these interventions change.</li><li>Family work cannot change genetic risk.</li><li>Language is not the target.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>EE is measured with the Camberwell Family Interview.</li><li>Indian families often show lower EE, which may help explain better outcomes in some studies.</li><li>The WHO studies reported better outcomes in developing countries.</li><li>Simultanagnosia, optic ataxia and oculomotor apraxia together make up:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in J&amp;K 2023, Gurugram. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 80, "question": "Simultanagnosia, optic ataxia and oculomotor apraxia together make up:", "options": ["Balint’s syndrome", "Gerstmann syndrome", "Anton’s syndrome", "Klüver–Bucy syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Balint’s syndrome (1909) has three features: inability to see more than one object at a time (simultanagnosia), poor visually guided reaching (optic ataxia), and difficulty directing gaze voluntarily (oculomotor apraxia). It follows bilateral parieto-occipital damage.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Gerstmann syndrome involves finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia and right–left confusion.</li><li>Anton’s syndrome is cortical blindness with denial.</li><li>Klüver–Bucy syndrome follows bilateral temporal damage.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Balint’s syndrome is a disorder of the dorsal (‘where/how’) visual stream.</li><li>Object agnosias involve the ventral (‘what’) stream.</li><li>Posterior cortical atrophy (a variant of Alzheimer’s disease) can cause it.</li><li>Which two medicines have been approved by the US FDA for treating irritability (aggression, tantrums, self-injury) in children with autism?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this exact point has not been asked directly yet, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics. It may come in your exam in a new form — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 81, "question": "Which two medicines have been approved by the US FDA for treating irritability (aggression, tantrums, self-injury) in children with autism?", "options": ["Lithium and valproate", "Methylphenidate and atomoxetine", "Risperidone and aripiprazole", "Fluoxetine and sertraline"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Risperidone (2006) and aripiprazole (2009) were approved for irritability associated with autism. No medicine treats the core social-communication features of autism.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Methylphenidate and atomoxetine treat ADHD symptoms.</li><li>SSRIs are used for anxiety or repetitive behaviours, without approval for irritability.</li><li>Lithium and valproate are mood stabilisers.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Weight gain and metabolic problems are key side effects of these medicines.</li><li>Behavioural interventions remain the main treatment for autism.</li><li>Melatonin is often used for sleep problems in autism.</li><li>Arrange these landmarks in neuropsychology in the order in which they happened:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 82, "question": "Arrange these landmarks in neuropsychology in the order in which they happened: I. Wernicke describes sensory (receptive) aphasia II. Broca describes the patient ‘Tan’ with loss of speech III. Sperry’s split-brain research IV. Scoville and Milner report patient H.M.", "options": ["I, II, IV, III", "II, I, IV, III", "II, I, III, IV", "II, IV, I, III"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Broca reported his patient ‘Tan’ in 1861; Wernicke described sensory aphasia in 1874; Scoville and Milner reported H.M. in 1957; and Roger Sperry’s split-brain research of the 1960s won the Nobel Prize in 1981.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Broca’s report (1861) came before Wernicke’s (1874), and H.M. (1957) came before the split-brain work.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Broca’s patient Leborgne could say only ‘tan’.</li><li>Sperry shared the 1981 Nobel Prize with Hubel and Wiesel.</li><li>Gazzaniga continued split-brain work with Sperry.</li><li>To teach a non-verbal child to say ‘ball’, the therapist first rewards any sound, then only ‘b’ sounds, then ‘ba’, and finally only the full word. This technique is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 83, "question": "To teach a non-verbal child to say ‘ball’, the therapist first rewards any sound, then only ‘b’ sounds, then ‘ba’, and finally only the full word. This technique is:", "options": ["Shaping", "Flooding", "Fading", "Chaining"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Shaping (successive approximation) reinforces responses that come closer and closer to the target behaviour, while earlier, rougher attempts are no longer reinforced.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Chaining links separate steps into a sequence.</li><li>Fading gradually removes prompts.</li><li>Flooding is intense exposure to a feared stimulus.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>B.F. Skinner showed shaping with pigeons and rats.</li><li>Shaping is widely used in speech and self-help training.</li><li>It needs consistent reinforcement of each new step.</li><li>Patient H.M. had no memory of ever practising a mirror-drawing task, yet his performance improved day after day. This shows that:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, RML 2024, IHBAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 84, "question": "Patient H.M. had no memory of ever practising a mirror-drawing task, yet his performance improved day after day. This shows that:", "options": ["His retrograde memory was more damaged than his anterograde memory", "Motor learning depends on the medial temporal lobes", "Short-term memory depends on the hippocampus", "Procedural (implicit) memory can be spared when declarative memory is lost"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> After bilateral removal of the medial temporal lobes (Scoville, 1953), H.M. could not form new declarative memories, but Brenda Milner showed he could still learn motor skills. Procedural memory relies on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, not the hippocampus.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>His main problem was severe anterograde amnesia.</li><li>His short-term memory (digit span) was normal.</li><li>His motor learning was intact even without the medial temporal lobes.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>H.M. = Henry Molaison (1926–2008).</li><li>Squire separated declarative (explicit) and non-declarative (implicit) memory.</li><li>JANAS asked which structure’s injury causes anterograde amnesia (hippocampus).</li><li>In a dementia ward, staff use large boards showing the day, date, weather and next meal, and repeatedly remind patients of where they are. This approach is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, GBU 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 85, "question": "In a dementia ward, staff use large boards showing the day, date, weather and next meal, and repeatedly remind patients of where they are. This approach is:", "options": ["Reality orientation", "Token economy", "Reminiscence therapy", "Validation therapy"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Reality orientation (Folsom, 1966) presents orientation information again and again — by staff and on boards — to reduce confusion in people with dementia.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Reminiscence therapy uses past memories, photos and objects.</li><li>Validation therapy accepts the person’s reality and responds to feelings.</li><li>A token economy rewards target behaviours with tokens.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Cognitive stimulation therapy is a modern, evidence-based successor.</li><li>Rigid correction can upset the person, so a gentle approach is used.</li><li>Validation therapy was developed by Naomi Feil.</li><li>After a stroke, Mr. Rao eats food only from the right half of his plate and, when asked to draw a clock, puts all the numbers on the right side. The lesion is most likely in the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 86, "question": "After a stroke, Mr. Rao eats food only from the right half of his plate and, when asked to draw a clock, puts all the numbers on the right side. The lesion is most likely in the:", "options": ["Left parietal lobe", "Right parietal lobe", "Right occipital lobe", "Left frontal lobe"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Ignoring the left side of space is hemispatial neglect. It is most common and most severe after damage to the right parietal lobe (especially the inferior parietal lobule).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Left parietal damage rarely causes lasting neglect.</li><li>Occipital damage causes a visual field loss, but the person knows about and compensates for it.</li><li>Left frontal damage usually affects speech and movement.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Line bisection and cancellation tests are used to check neglect.</li><li>Neglect often occurs with anosognosia for left-sided weakness.</li><li>RINPAS 2024 used spatial neglect as an option.</li><li>In the ‘Individual Placement and Support’ (IPS) model of supported employment for people with severe mental illness:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RML 2024, RINPAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 87, "question": "In the ‘Individual Placement and Support’ (IPS) model of supported employment for people with severe mental illness:", "options": ["Work is offered only in sheltered workshops", "People must finish long pre-vocational training before any job", "People are placed quickly in competitive jobs and then supported on the job", "Employment is delayed until all symptoms disappear"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> IPS (Becker and Drake) follows ‘place, then train’: rapid job search for real, competitive jobs based on the person’s choice, with ongoing support. It gets better job results than ‘train, then place’ models.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Long pre-vocational training is the older approach.</li><li>Sheltered workshops are separate, non-competitive settings.</li><li>IPS does not wait for full symptom remission.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Supported employment is a core part of psychosocial rehabilitation.</li><li>GMCH 2023 asked what the main aim of psychological rehabilitation is.</li><li>Clubhouse models (Fountain House) are another approach.</li><li>The primary auditory cortex (Heschl’s gyrus) is located in the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, RML 2024, J&amp;K 2023 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 88, "question": "The primary auditory cortex (Heschl’s gyrus) is located in the:", "options": ["Occipital lobe", "Parietal lobe", "Frontal lobe", "Temporal lobe"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Heschl’s transverse temporal gyri (Brodmann areas 41 and 42), on the upper surface of the temporal lobe, form the primary auditory cortex.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The parietal lobe holds the primary somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus).</li><li>The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex.</li><li>The frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus).</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RINPAS 2024: ‘The ___ lobe is to hearing as the occipital lobe is to vision.’</li><li>Wernicke’s area lies just behind the auditory cortex.</li><li>IHBAS 2025 and RINPAS 2019 asked which lobe is responsible for vision.</li><li>After throwing food on the floor, Kiran must clean up the mess and then also clean the whole dining area. This procedure is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 89, "question": "After throwing food on the floor, Kiran must clean up the mess and then also clean the whole dining area. This procedure is:", "options": ["Restitutional overcorrection", "Negative reinforcement", "Time-out", "Response cost"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Overcorrection (Foxx and Azrin, 1972) has two forms: restitution (restoring the environment to better than before, as here) and positive practice (repeatedly practising the correct behaviour).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Time-out removes access to reinforcement for a short period.</li><li>Response cost removes a reinforcer such as tokens.</li><li>Negative reinforcement increases a behaviour by removing something unpleasant.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Foxx and Azrin also developed rapid toilet training.</li><li>Overcorrection is used in ID and autism settings.</li><li>Positive practice: e.g. walking down the corridor slowly ten times after running.</li><li>Which test is most sensitive for detecting subtle frontal lobe executive dysfunction in early neurodegenerative conditions?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 90, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IMHANS 2025</span>Which test is most sensitive for detecting subtle frontal lobe executive dysfunction in early neurodegenerative conditions?", "options": ["Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST)", "Benton Visual Retention Test (BVRT)", "Rey–Osterrieth Complex Figure Test (ROCF)", "Finger Tapping Test"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The WCST measures abstract concept formation and set-shifting: the person must find the sorting rule from feedback and switch when it changes. Perseverative errors are a sensitive sign of frontal executive dysfunction.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>BVRT mainly tests visual memory and visuoconstruction.</li><li>ROCF tests visuoconstruction (copy) and visual memory (recall).</li><li>Finger tapping tests simple motor speed.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>WCST was developed by Grant and Berg (1948).</li><li>Main scores: categories completed and perseverative errors.</li><li>Other executive tests: Trail Making B, Tower of London, verbal fluency.</li><li>Down syndrome is most commonly caused by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This is an actual previous-year question (IMHANS 2025). Examiners do repeat questions, sometimes word for word, and this area also appeared in M.Phil Entrance 2021. Make sure you can answer it instantly — and keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 91, "question": "Down syndrome is most commonly caused by:", "options": ["An extra X chromosome in a male", "An extra copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy 21)", "A missing X chromosome", "A deletion on chromosome 15"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> About 95% of Down syndrome cases are due to trisomy 21, usually from non-disjunction during egg formation; risk rises with maternal age.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A missing X chromosome causes Turner syndrome (45,X).</li><li>An extra X in a male (47,XXY) causes Klinefelter syndrome.</li><li>Chromosome 15 deletions cause Prader–Willi or Angelman syndrome.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>John Langdon Down described it in 1866.</li><li>Chromosome 21 carries the APP gene, so adults with Down syndrome develop Alzheimer’s disease early.</li><li>Other forms: translocation and mosaicism.</li><li>A patient’s speech is halting and effortful, but he understands well and can repeat long sentences perfectly. This pattern fits:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in IHBAS 2025, JEMAS 2024, M.Phil Entrance 2021. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 92, "question": "A patient’s speech is halting and effortful, but he understands well and can repeat long sentences perfectly. This pattern fits:", "options": ["Broca’s aphasia", "Conduction aphasia", "Global aphasia", "Transcortical motor aphasia"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Transcortical motor aphasia looks like Broca’s aphasia (non-fluent, good comprehension), but repetition is preserved because the perisylvian language circuit is spared. The lesion lies in front of or above Broca’s area.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>In Broca’s aphasia repetition is also poor.</li><li>Conduction aphasia is fluent.</li><li>Global aphasia has poor comprehension and poor repetition.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Transcortical sensory aphasia = fluent, poor comprehension, good repetition (often echolalic).</li><li>Mixed transcortical = ‘isolation of the speech area’.</li><li>These often follow watershed (border-zone) strokes.</li><li>To teach Ravi, who has intellectual disability, to put on his shirt, the therapist does all the steps except the last one, which Ravi completes and is praised for. Gradually Ravi is asked to do the last two, then the last three steps. This is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, RML 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 93, "question": "To teach Ravi, who has intellectual disability, to put on his shirt, the therapist does all the steps except the last one, which Ravi completes and is praised for. Gradually Ravi is asked to do the last two, then the last three steps. This is:", "options": ["Forward chaining", "Shaping", "Backward chaining", "Overcorrection"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Therapies & interventions", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In backward chaining, the last step is taught first, so the learner always finishes the task and gets the natural reward of completion. Earlier steps are added one by one.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Forward chaining teaches the first step first.</li><li>Shaping rewards closer versions of one behaviour, not a sequence of steps.</li><li>Overcorrection is a mild punishment procedure.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Task analysis breaks a skill into small steps before chaining.</li><li>Total-task presentation teaches all steps in each session.</li><li>Chaining is common in self-help skills training for ID and autism.</li><li>Seven-year-old Meena says ‘wabbit’ for rabbit and ‘tat’ for cat. Her vocabulary, sentences and understanding are normal for her age. The most likely diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in RML 2022, JEPAS 2020. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 94, "question": "Seven-year-old Meena says ‘wabbit’ for rabbit and ‘tat’ for cat. Her vocabulary, sentences and understanding are normal for her age. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Language disorder", "Speech sound disorder", "Childhood-onset fluency disorder", "Social (pragmatic) communication disorder"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Speech sound disorder (formerly phonological disorder) is persistent difficulty producing speech sounds, beyond what is expected for age, with otherwise normal language.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Language disorder involves reduced vocabulary, limited sentences or poor comprehension.</li><li>Fluency disorder (stuttering) affects the flow of speech.</li><li>Pragmatic disorder affects the social use of language.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Many sound errors are normal in preschoolers; persistence beyond age expectations matters.</li><li>Speech-language therapists treat it.</li><li>All four are ‘communication disorders’ in DSM-5.</li><li>Asked to ‘show how you wave goodbye’, Mr. Iqbal cannot do it, though he understands the request and waves normally when his son leaves. His arm is not weak. This is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GBU 2025, RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2022. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 95, "question": "Asked to ‘show how you wave goodbye’, Mr. Iqbal cannot do it, though he understands the request and waves normally when his son leaves. His arm is not weak. This is:", "options": ["Constructional apraxia", "Dressing apraxia", "Ideational apraxia", "Ideomotor apraxia"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Apraxia is inability to carry out learned, purposeful movements despite intact strength and understanding. In ideomotor apraxia, gestures fail on command but may appear automatically. It usually follows left parietal or frontal damage.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Ideational apraxia is a failure in the sequence or concept of a multi-step action (e.g. making tea).</li><li>Constructional apraxia is difficulty copying drawings or building block designs.</li><li>Dressing apraxia is difficulty putting on clothes, often after right parietal damage.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Liepmann (1900) first classified the apraxias.</li><li>Apraxia ≠ ataxia (incoordination due to cerebellar damage).</li><li>RML 2024 asked to choose between aphasia, agnosia, apraxia and ataxia after a stroke.</li><li>For the last 5 months, 9-year-old Tanmay has had sudden eye-blinking and throat-clearing. He has no other illness. The most appropriate DSM-5 diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GBU 2025, RML 2024, RINPAS 2024. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 96, "question": "For the last 5 months, 9-year-old Tanmay has had sudden eye-blinking and throat-clearing. He has no other illness. The most appropriate DSM-5 diagnosis is:", "options": ["Tourette’s disorder", "Persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder", "Provisional tic disorder", "Obsessive-compulsive disorder"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Tics present for less than 1 year are diagnosed as provisional tic disorder. Tourette’s disorder needs multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year, as does persistent tic disorder (motor or vocal only).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Tourette’s needs more than 1 year.</li><li>Persistent tic disorder needs more than 1 year.</li><li>Compulsions are purposeful acts to reduce anxiety; tics are sudden and non-rhythmic.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 replaced ‘transient tic disorder’ with ‘provisional tic disorder’.</li><li>Onset must be before age 18.</li><li>Habit reversal training (CBIT) is the main psychological treatment.</li><li>Gerstmann syndrome, linked to damage to the left angular gyrus, includes all of the following, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, NFSU 2023 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 97, "question": "Gerstmann syndrome, linked to damage to the left angular gyrus, includes all of the following, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Finger agnosia", "Left-sided hemispatial neglect", "Acalculia", "Right–left disorientation"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Gerstmann syndrome has four features: finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia and right–left disorientation. It follows lesions of the dominant (left) parietal lobe, around the angular gyrus.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Finger agnosia is a core feature.</li><li>Acalculia (difficulty with calculation) is a core feature.</li><li>Right–left disorientation is a core feature.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The fourth feature is agraphia (difficulty writing).</li><li>Neglect follows right parietal damage, not left.</li><li>Josef Gerstmann described it in 1924.</li><li>On an intelligence test, Raju has a mental age of 6 years and a chronological age of 10 years. His ratio IQ and the ICD-10 level of intellectual disability are:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this area has appeared in GBU 2025, NFSU 2023. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 98, "question": "On an intelligence test, Raju has a mental age of 6 years and a chronological age of 10 years. His ratio IQ and the ICD-10 level of intellectual disability are:", "options": ["167 — very superior", "60 — moderate", "60 — mild", "40 — moderate"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Ratio IQ = (Mental age ÷ Chronological age) × 100 = (6 ÷ 10) × 100 = 60. In ICD-10, mild intellectual disability is IQ 50–69.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Moderate is IQ 35–49.</li><li>40 comes from a calculation error.</li><li>167 comes from dividing CA by MA.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-10 ranges: mild 50–69, moderate 35–49, severe 20–34, profound below 20.</li><li>DSM-5 rates severity by adaptive functioning, not IQ.</li><li>Ratio IQ was introduced by William Stern (1912) and used by Terman (1916).</li><li>The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 99, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · JEMAS 2024</span>The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "options": ["Verbal fluency", "Visual perception", "Memory", "Attention"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Neuropsychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In the Stroop test the person names the ink colour of a colour word printed in a different colour (the word ‘RED’ in blue ink). Reading is automatic, so suppressing it needs selective attention and response inhibition.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Verbal fluency is tested with word-generation tasks (FAS test).</li><li>Basic visual perception is intact; the problem is interference.</li><li>No material has to be stored or recalled.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Described by J. Ridley Stroop (1935).</li><li>Slowing on the interference card is linked with frontal (anterior cingulate) dysfunction.</li><li>RML 2024 asked it as ‘Stroop effect is an account of…’</li><li>Ten-year-old Pooja daydreams in class, loses her books, makes careless mistakes and forgets instructions at home and school. She is not restless or impulsive. The most likely diagnosis is ADHD:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "This is an actual previous-year question (JEMAS 2024). Examiners do repeat questions, sometimes word for word, and this area also appeared in GBU 2025, RML 2024, IHBAS 2024. Make sure you can answer it instantly — and keep the points above ready."}, {"id": 100, "question": "Ten-year-old Pooja daydreams in class, loses her books, makes careless mistakes and forgets instructions at home and school. She is not restless or impulsive. The most likely diagnosis is ADHD:", "options": ["Combined presentation", "Predominantly hyperactive-impulsive presentation", "In partial remission", "Predominantly inattentive presentation"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 describes three ADHD presentations. Pooja has many inattention symptoms in two settings but no hyperactivity or impulsivity, so she fits the predominantly inattentive presentation — often missed, especially in girls.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The hyperactive-impulsive presentation needs restlessness and impulsivity.</li><li>The combined presentation needs both sets of symptoms.</li><li>‘In partial remission’ is used when full criteria were met before but not now.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>At least 6 symptoms are needed (5 for age 17 and older).</li><li>Symptoms must be present before age 12 and in at least two settings.</li><li>DSM-IV called these ‘subtypes’; DSM-5 calls them ‘presentations’.</li><li>— End of Mock 10 —</li><li>Next: Mocks 11–15 — full-length papers.  Queries: team@psychlogic.in</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic’s thorough analysis, this is a high-frequency area — recent papers such as IHBAS 2025, GBU 2025, JEMAS 2024 have asked it. Examiners clearly like it, so it may well come in your exam too — keep the points above ready."}];
